      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Country Club Plaza was named after the Country Club District, a neighborhood developed by J.C. Nichols.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Country_Club_Plaza",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Its design blends Baroque and Moorish Revival styles, echoing Seville, Spain, and features 40 statues, fountains, and 12 towers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Country_Club_Plaza",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "When first announced, the project was dubbed 'Nichols' Folly' before becoming a nationally celebrated landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Country_Club_Plaza",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A fountain by Henri-Léon Gréber showcases four rearing horses, a signature piece of the Plaza.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
